Cador: warn when ENTMAKEX accepts an INSERT rejected by AutoCAD and BricsCAD

== Symptom ==

Cador accepts this incomplete INSERT and returns an ename without a warning:

    (entmakex
      '((0 . "INSERT")
        (2 . "SCHME_TEST_C1")
        (8 . "0")
        (66 . 1)
        (10 0.0 0.0 0.0)))

No block named `SCHME_TEST_C1` has been defined, and group 66 announces an
attribute sequence although no ATTRIB/SEQEND sequence follows. AutoCAD and
BricsCAD reject the same data. This made a SCHME+ C1 test pass with Cador and
fail on both real CAD jobs:

- AutoCAD: Fabrik job 511433, `test-cad:autocad:windows`;
- BricsCAD: Fabrik job 511434, `test-cad:bricscad:windows`.

The SCHME+ test has been corrected to stop creating an invalid INSERT, but the
permissive Cador behaviour remains dangerous: it hides invalid DXF fixtures
and production code until the suite reaches a real CAD.

== Expected behaviour ==

Preferred: under the AutoCAD and BricsCAD dialects, `host-entmakex` validates
the immediately checkable INSERT invariants and returns nil as the vendors do.
At least the referenced group-2 block name must resolve to a BLOCK_RECORD.

If Cador deliberately keeps a permissive construction mode, accepting this
entity must emit a registered dialect warning on stderr, for example:

    [entmakex-invalid-insert] Cador accepted an INSERT rejected by AutoCAD and BricsCAD: block SCHME_TEST_C1 is undefined and group 66 announces an unfinished attribute sequence.

The tag must follow the standard dialect-warning catalogue so consumers can
extract it with:

    ^\[[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)+\](?: |:).*$

== Acceptance ==

1. Add Cador regression tests for an INSERT whose block name is absent and for
   group 66 without a completed ATTRIB/SEQEND sequence.
2. Measure and encode the AutoCAD/BricsCAD return contract (nil, no entity
   added to the database).
3. Prefer matching that contract under `--dialect autocad-*` and
   `--dialect bricscad-*`.
4. If a permissive Clautolisp dialect continues to return an ename, register
   and emit `[entmakex-invalid-insert]`, with an entry in the generated dialect
   warnings annex.
5. Verify that no invalid entity becomes visible through ENTGET, ENTLAST or
   ENTNEXT when the vendor-compatible path returns nil.
